5️⃣ Stone Game IV (Hard – DP + Game Theory)
🔹 Problem Statement

Alice and Bob are playing a game with a pile of n stones.

Alice starts first.

On each turn, a player can remove any perfect square number of stones
(1, 4, 9, 16, 25, …)

The player who cannot make a move loses.

Return true if Alice can win assuming both play optimally, else false.

🔹 Example 1
Input: n = 1
Output: true

Alice removes 1 → wins.

🔹 Example 2
Input: n = 2
Output: false

Alice removes 1 → leaves 1
Bob removes 1 → wins
So Alice loses.

🔹 Example 3
Input: n = 4
Output: true

Alice removes 4 → wins instantly.

🔎 Intuition

This is a Game DP / Win-Loss State problem.

Key idea:

If there exists any move that makes opponent lose →
current player wins.

So we define:

dp[i] = true  → winning position
dp[i] = false → losing position
💡 Transition Formula

For each i from 1 to n:

Check all perfect squares:

If there exists square s such that:
dp[i - s] == false
→ dp[i] = true

Because we force opponent into losing position.

If none works:

dp[i] = false
⚙️ Algorithm

1️⃣ Create boolean array dp[n+1]
2️⃣ For each i:

Check all perfect squares ≤ i

If dp[i - square] == false → mark dp[i] = true

Return dp[n]

💻 Java Code
class Solution {
    public boolean winnerSquareGame(int n) {
        
        boolean[] dp = new boolean[n + 1];
        
        for (int i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
            
            for (int k = 1; k * k <= i; k++) {
                
                if (!dp[i - k * k]) {
                    dp[i] = true;
                    break;
                }
            }
        }
        
        return dp[n];
    }
}
🧠 Code Explanation
Step 1: Base Case
dp[0] = false

If 0 stones → lose.

Step 2: Build from Bottom-Up

